35,2,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
36,2,,status,Kenji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
37,2,,status,Minako,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
38,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
39,2,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
40,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"It looks like we have some challenges. The earth-moving firms are worried about clay and slippage, and we can't hoard the center or main gate without closing them.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The crossing counts show people stick to firm, open ground for the tram stop, and drainage issues have doubled since the culvert was capped, alongside high August heat.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Minato,,"The groundskeeping staff has dropped to seven people, so we rely on bought-in services, but there are two volunteer groups already helping with habitat and youth sports.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The earlier overflow basin is still in great shape, but the civic hall apron has been patched three times. Also, half the eastern avenue trees died due to lack of watering.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The habitat fund only covers planting and water management, and the upkeep costs for those areas are now triple that of hard-surfaced ground.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Jonathan, that's a big difference in cost! If the fund only pays for planting, does that mean we can't afford any buildings or paved areas?","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, it seems we are caught between a financial preference for hard surfaces and a funding source that explicitly forbids them. That is a precarious position.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Minato,,I wonder if the volunteer groups could help with the watering that Minako mentioned? Maybe they can help keep the plants alive so we don't lose them again.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, while volunteers are helpful, the eastern avenue failure was a contractual oversight. We need a plan that is structurally sound and realistically maintained, not one that relies on goodwill.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"Exactly. The fund covers the initial planting, but the upkeep ledger shows that the long-term cost of maintaining those green spaces is far higher than for paved areas. We must be cautious.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"If we can't hoard the center, maybe we should pick a plan that doesn't require huge amounts of digging in the middle? I don't want the park closed!","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, that aligns with the crossing data. If the center is the primary thoroughfare to the tram, any plan requiring extensive central excavation would be a disaster for the daily commuters.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I wonder if the sports terraces or the market plaza would be easier to maintain with the few staff we have left, since they use more hard surfaces.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, the civic hall apron's failure suggests that hard surfaces aren't automatically low-maintenance. We need a design that is durable, not just one that avoids watering.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I must emphasize that the habitat fund's restrictions are absolute. If we choose a plan heavy on surfacing, like the plaza or terraces, we lose the primary source of capital funding.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"Wait, so we have to choose between something we can't afford to build or something we can't afford to keep? This is getting really complicated!","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,It is a classic dilemma. We must weigh the immediate capital availability of the habitat fund against the long-term operational costs and the actual behavior of the park's users.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Minato,,I just want to make sure we don't pick something that the seven staff members can't handle. Maybe the volunteer groups can help with the habitat parts?,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, the fact that a habitat volunteer group already exists is a strong point. It suggests a sustainable way to manage the higher upkeep costs Jonathan mentioned.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minato"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"That is a fair point, Minako. If we can leverage existing volunteer passion to offset the maintenance burden, the habitat fund becomes a much more viable path forward.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
61,3,,status,Kenji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
